-- title:
--   Lattices, and cone types favoured by majority in a cube
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. The **rescaled lattice** of mesh $h$ is
--
--   $$h\mathbb{Z}^d \;=\; \{x \in \mathbb{R}^d \ :\ \forall i,\ \exists n \in \mathbb{Z},\ x_i = n h\},$$
--
--   and the **lattice** is the case $h = 1$.
--
--   Given a configuration $\Gamma$ and a double cone $V$ (as a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$), the part of the open cube $A_h(u)$ on which $\Gamma$ dominates $V$ is
--
--   $$A^V_h(u) \;=\; \{x \in A_h(u) \ :\ V \subseteq \Gamma(x)\},$$
--
--   the points of the cube whose own cone contains $V$. Given moreover a finite family of reference cones for $\Gamma$ with common apex angle $\theta$ — a finite set of unit axes such that every $\Gamma(x)$ contains the cone of aperture $\theta$ about one of them — an axis $v$ is **$h$-favoured by majority at $u$** when $v$ is one of those axes and
--
--   $$\lambda_d\big(A^{V(w,\theta)}_h(u)\big) \;\le\; \lambda_d\big(A^{V(v,\theta)}_h(u)\big) \qquad \text{for every axis } w \text{ of the family},$$
--
--   i.e. among the finitely many reference cones, the one about $v$ is the one whose dominating set fills the largest part of the cube.
--
--   Majority selection is the device that collapses a configuration, which may vary from point to point inside a cube, to a single cone attached to the cube; since the reference family is finite, the favoured axis fills at least a $1/L$ fraction of the cube, which is the quantitative content the later counting uses.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Volumes are `ℝ≥0∞`-valued, so the maximality condition compares extended reals and is meaningful even when the sets are non-measurable in principle (their measurability is supplied elsewhere, from an explicit measurability hypothesis on $\Gamma$). The cubes here are the *open* ones. The file also records that a literal reading of the source's comparison lemma with $x,y \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ rather than $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ is false, with an explicit one-dimensional counterexample, and that the source's thin-cone lemma fails in dimension one at the radius where it is applied, because there every double cone is all of $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$.
